  <data name="HelpTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Help</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HelpSubtitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Learn how to read storage usage, drill into large folders, understand cleanup safety, and configure automation.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HelpQuickStartTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Quick start</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HelpQuickStartDescription.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Storage Lens is designed as a read → investigate → review workflow.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HelpQuickStartStepOne.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>1. Start on Overview and compare used, free, and reviewable space.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HelpQuickStartStepTwo.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>2. Click any category, largest item, or growing folder to open it in Storage Browser.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HelpQuickStartStepThree.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>3. Open Cleanup Review, inspect every matching file in each folder tree, uncheck anything you want to keep, and confirm the cleanup.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HelpCategoriesTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Reading categories</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HelpCategoriesDescription.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Categories group storage by purpose: apps, video, photos, system, documents, temporary data, or other. Click a category tile to filter all matching items across the selected drive.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HelpDrillDownTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Drilling into folders</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HelpDrillDownDescription.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Select a folder from the tree, or click a folder row in the item list, to show its direct children. Use the up button to return to its parent.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HelpGrowthTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Finding growing folders</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HelpGrowthDescription.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Storage Lens keeps the first indexed size for every folder. Growth is shown from that baseline, deleted folders are removed, and recreated folders start fresh. Change the tracked-folder minimum in Automation; it starts at 1 GB.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HelpCleanupSafetyTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Cleanup safety</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HelpCleanupSafetyDescription.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Safe, Review, and Caution are separate trust levels. Click a recommendation to inspect every candidate page. Confirmed cleanup moves eligible items to the Recycle Bin; protected and changed files are skipped.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HelpMonitoringTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Background monitoring</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HelpMonitoringDescription.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enable monitoring in Automation for low-space and folder-growth alerts.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HelpSchedulesTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Scheduled cleanup reviews</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HelpSchedulesDescription.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Create daily, weekly, biweekly, or monthly category reviews. Review-only schedules send a notification; automatic cleanup moves only still-eligible files to the Recycle Bin.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HelpSafetyInfoBar.Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Your files stay yours</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HelpSafetyInfoBar.Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Storage analysis is indexed locally on this device. Background monitoring reads drives and tracked folders; cleanup only moves eligible items to the Recycle Bin after validation.</value>
  </data>
